+## FAQ
+
+### What are the best open source AI tools for healthcare in 2026?
+For general breadth, start with MONAI, nnU-Net, TorchXRayVision, medspacy, and DeepChem. They cover the strongest open ecosystems across imaging, clinical text, and translational discovery.
+
+### Are these healthcare AI tools really open source and self-hostable?
+Yes. Every entry in this list points to a public GitHub repository. Some are research-heavy rather than turnkey products, but none are proprietary SaaS listings.
+
+### Which open source healthcare AI tools are best for medical imaging?
+MONAI, nnU-Net, MedSAM, TorchIO, and TorchXRayVision are the strongest starting points depending on whether you care most about end-to-end framework support, segmentation quality, prompt-based segmentation, preprocessing, or chest X-rays.
+
+### What is the best open source clinical NLP stack for healthcare notes?
+medspacy is the most practical first stop for many teams. CogStack NLP is relevant when you want a broader health-data workflow context, while smaller utilities like PyRuSH and QuickUMLS help fill common pipeline gaps.
+
+### Are there enough mature open source pathology AI repositories on GitHub?
+Not really. Compared with imaging, open pathology tooling is still thinner and more research-code-centric. STAMP, CLAM, and TRIDENT are strong repos, but this remains a genuine ecosystem gap.
+
+### Which GitHub repos are best for drug discovery or translational healthcare AI?
+DeepChem is the broadest general framework, while scGPT, BioReason, IntegrAO, and AGILE are stronger if your roadmap includes single-cell biology, multimodal reasoning, omics integration, or therapeutic design.
+
+### How should I choose between research repos and production-ready healthcare AI tools?
+Use frameworks like MONAI, TorchIO, and medspacy when you need reusable building blocks. Use project repos like MedRAX, TRIDENT, or AGILE when you are comfortable adapting research code for internal use.
+
